WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.562
Welcome back to Agentic C sharp In the
introduction we separated three
responsibilities

00:00:05.812 --> 00:00:10.775
the operational system the Agent Framework
that coordinates model and tool interaction

00:00:11.163 --> 00:00:14.812
and Foundry as the platform We also followed
a real request in a

00:00:14.825 --> 00:00:19.488
debugger The model selected a function tool
ordinary C sharp code read the

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:23.662
Energy Hub and the returned facts became
context for the next model call

00:00:24.025 --> 00:00:28.075
The agent answered but the lamp did not
change Here is our continuing

00:00:28.088 --> 00:00:32.775
incident L-417 is on in daylight Before we
give an agent a way

00:00:32.787 --> 00:00:36.750
to request a repair we need to understand
the operation itself and how

00:00:36.762 --> 00:00:39.262
to prove its result That is our first lab

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:45.125
We are now in Visual Studio Code on macOS
The repository is the

00:00:45.137 --> 00:00:49.775
same cumulative C sharp solution We select a
runtime stage we do not

00:00:49.787 --> 00:00:53.850
maintain a different branch for each lesson
Start with the README then locate

00:00:53.862 --> 00:00:59.700
Apps Services Contracts the Aspire AppHost
and Tests Apps contains the two web

00:00:59.712 --> 00:01:04.675
interfaces Services contains the operational
services and the agent services

00:01:05.037 --> 00:01:10.225
Each boundary has focused contracts AppHost
starts and connects the local processes

00:01:10.587 --> 00:01:15.150
The tests check behavior that must remain
reliable as we add intelligence The

00:01:15.162 --> 00:01:19.650
lab code is dot net eleven The same Agent
Framework concepts and patterns

00:01:19.662 --> 00:01:24.150
apply on dot net ten LTS but this checkout
needs the SDK declared

00:01:24.162 --> 00:01:29.100
here Check the version before
troubleshooting application errors C sharp
Dev

00:01:29.112 --> 00:01:33.462
Kit supplies the C sharp development
experience in VS Code On a Mac

00:01:33.475 --> 00:01:38.375
we use VS Code on Windows you can also
choose Visual Studio 2026

00:01:38.738 --> 00:01:42.950
We already introduced debugging in Unit zero
Today we will use the editor

00:01:42.975 --> 00:01:45.012
to follow the deterministic command path

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:51.550
In the introduction we visited Foundry in a
browser Foundry Toolkit brings supported

00:01:51.562 --> 00:01:56.175
project resources into VS Code so we can
orient ourselves without leaving the

00:01:56.188 --> 00:02:00.663
editor Here is our existing project and here
is the hosted Operations Agent

00:02:01.025 --> 00:02:05.300
These are cloud resources opening this view
does not create another local agent

00:02:05.662 --> 00:02:09.825
The portal and Toolkit are clients for the
same platform although their available

00:02:09.838 --> 00:02:14.712
views and features are not identical This
distinction matters for today's exercise

00:02:15.075 --> 00:02:19.138
We are about to operate the local
deterministic system We are not invoking

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:23.638
this hosted agent and the exercise needs no
model credentials If you do

00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:28.550
not have access to a Foundry project follow
the recorded orientation and continue

00:02:28.562 --> 00:02:32.312
with the local lab We will return to hosting
when the architecture needs

00:02:32.325 --> 00:02:37.562
it For now remember the division the editor
is our development environment Foundry

00:02:37.575 --> 00:02:42.338
supplies platform services and the Energy
Hub remains responsible for the operational

00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:43.825
state we are about to change

00:02:45.333 --> 00:02:48.096
By the end of this module you should be able
to trace one

00:02:48.108 --> 00:02:52.221
command from the operator interface to the
Hub and controller distinguish an

00:02:52.233 --> 00:02:56.871
intended state from a confirmed state and
prove both success and failure You

00:02:56.883 --> 00:03:00.308
should also be able to reset the lab for
another attempt This is

00:03:00.321 --> 00:03:04.596
why we start with deterministic code
Restoring a known schedule is a bounded

00:03:04.608 --> 00:03:09.246
operation with explicit rules A language
model does not improve the definition

00:03:09.258 --> 00:03:13.358
of success Later an agent may help
investigate why the lamp is on

00:03:13.671 --> 00:03:16.433
that does not transfer ownership of the
repair to the model

00:03:18.333 --> 00:03:23.321
Start the AppHost and use its printed
dashboard URL Aspire shows the processes

00:03:23.333 --> 00:03:27.883
and their endpoints Open the Director and
Command Center The Director prepares

00:03:27.896 --> 00:03:32.746
our fixture Command Center is the operator's
application Select the Deterministic

00:03:32.758 --> 00:03:37.008
stage and prepare Lights On Reported by a
Client Now read the walkthrough

00:03:37.021 --> 00:03:42.671
in the main application Check the facts
before acting L-417 is reported on

00:03:43.033 --> 00:03:46.758
It is daylight the scheduled target is off
and this fixture has a

00:03:46.771 --> 00:03:51.196
manual override with a healthy controller
The Hub read provides our independent

00:03:51.208 --> 00:03:55.608
starting checkpoint These are seeded
conditions for this exercise We are not

00:03:55.621 --> 00:03:59.996
concluding that every daylight anomaly is an
abandoned override In the later

00:04:00.008 --> 00:04:03.471
investigation lessons explanations will need
their own evidence

00:04:05.333 --> 00:04:09.858
Before we click notice what Restore
Scheduled Mode means restore the schedule

00:04:10.171 --> 00:04:14.108
not set an arbitrary Boolean selected by a
model The Hub reads the

00:04:14.121 --> 00:04:18.446
controller state and computes the scheduled
target It records a pending command

00:04:18.508 --> 00:04:22.546
and sends the request through its controller
boundary Only a confirmed result

00:04:22.558 --> 00:04:26.596
allows it to update the reported state We
will locate the pending transition

00:04:26.608 --> 00:04:31.083
in the code because this operation completes
too quickly to inspect that intermediate

00:04:31.096 --> 00:04:36.158
state in the interface Now the operator
requests Restore Scheduled Mode The response

00:04:36.171 --> 00:04:40.396
arrives and the reported state changes to
off Read the outcome in Command

00:04:40.408 --> 00:04:44.983
Center then verify it at the Hub The
interface is useful feedback but

00:04:44.996 --> 00:04:48.608
it is not our only evidence This discipline
will stay the same when

00:04:48.621 --> 00:04:53.021
an agent starts selecting tools a convincing
sentence is not a command receipt

00:04:54.533 --> 00:04:58.133
Read these fields with me Desired is off
that is the target we

00:04:58.146 --> 00:05:02.871
requested Reported is off that is the state
confirmed by the controller response

00:05:03.233 --> 00:05:08.021
Manual override is cleared and the latest
command receipt says Succeeded Follow

00:05:08.033 --> 00:05:11.958
its correlation identifier into the Hub
activity to connect the request with

00:05:11.971 --> 00:05:16.246
its outcome Do not compare an unrelated
earlier receipt with the new request

00:05:16.608 --> 00:05:20.546
Our physical device in this lab is a
simulator We have verified a

00:05:20.558 --> 00:05:24.771
real software command path and a real
response from that simulator we have

00:05:24.783 --> 00:05:29.346
not claimed to operate a streetlight in a
deployed city The architectural lesson

00:05:29.358 --> 00:05:33.633
is transferable Keep the source of truth
clear and make the confirmation boundary

00:05:33.646 --> 00:05:37.858
visible We have now moved from a requested
target to a confirmed outcome

00:05:38.121 --> 00:05:40.596
with evidence independent of the operator's
display

00:05:42.533 --> 00:05:46.121
We are switching to VS Code on Windows to
inspect the same code

00:05:46.508 --> 00:05:50.971
not to repeat the successful demonstration
Each machine runs its own local lab

00:05:51.333 --> 00:05:54.833
You can follow this entire lesson on either
platform Let us find the

00:05:54.846 --> 00:05:59.333
mechanism behind that result Command Center
receives the operator's request and

00:05:59.346 --> 00:06:04.846
calls its Energy Hub gateway The gateway
crosses the focused HTTP boundary carrying

00:06:04.858 --> 00:06:09.446
the correlation identifier Command Center
does not reach around the Hub and write

00:06:09.458 --> 00:06:15.021
the simulator directly Inside the Hub find
RestoreScheduledModeAsync The optional

00:06:15.033 --> 00:06:18.546
expected revision is checked under the same
lock as the new revision claim

00:06:18.908 --> 00:06:23.333
The method rereads physical state computes
the scheduled target and records a

00:06:23.346 --> 00:06:28.083
pending command Now follow the call to
SetLampStateAsync That is the controller

00:06:28.096 --> 00:06:32.621
boundary The confirmation guard below it is
the critical part the result must

00:06:32.633 --> 00:06:36.783
be Succeeded and must include the actual
state Only then does this path

00:06:36.796 --> 00:06:41.571
assign reported state and clear the override
A success label without confirmation

00:06:41.583 --> 00:06:46.483
is handled as failure A superseded operation
must not overwrite a newer revision

00:06:46.846 --> 00:06:51.296
These are ordinary C sharp rules The
language model is not responsible for

00:06:51.308 --> 00:06:55.896
remembering them When we later expose a
capability to an agent the deterministic

00:06:55.908 --> 00:07:00.283
service still enforces its own contract The
debugger and source view help us

00:07:00.296 --> 00:07:04.721
identify where an outcome becomes true
rather than trusting what the caller hoped

00:07:04.733 --> 00:07:05.208
would happen

00:07:06.733 --> 00:07:12.008
A successful demonstration is only half the
lesson On Windows attach Visual Studio

00:07:12.021 --> 00:07:16.246
2026 to the Energy Hub through the Director
This is an optional debugger

00:07:16.258 --> 00:07:20.271
choice you can inspect the same C sharp with
VS Code Set an

00:07:20.283 --> 00:07:24.808
ordinary breakpoint at the confirmation
guard Prepare Controller Fault return

00:07:24.821 --> 00:07:29.121
to Command Center and request Restore
Scheduled Mode The breakpoint has stopped

00:07:29.133 --> 00:07:34.896
after the controller call Expand command
Result in Locals Status is Failed Actual

00:07:34.908 --> 00:07:39.433
Is On is null The summary says the
controller rejected the command Step

00:07:39.446 --> 00:07:43.883
over the condition Execution skips the
successful update and reaches the failure

00:07:43.896 --> 00:07:48.333
path This is the value of debugging we can
connect the observed behavior

00:07:48.346 --> 00:07:52.408
to the exact rule that enforces it A
selected operation is not a

00:07:52.421 --> 00:07:56.983
confirmed repair There is another useful
observation in this take Holding the

00:07:56.996 --> 00:08:00.871
Hub at a breakpoint caused the caller to
time out After continuing the

00:08:00.883 --> 00:08:05.496
Hub still recorded the controller's failed
result A timeout alone does not establish

00:08:05.508 --> 00:08:09.908
the outcome Read the receipt before retrying
Now detach through the Director

00:08:10.271 --> 00:08:14.358
Having verified the previous failure make
another request without the debugger

00:08:14.371 --> 00:08:19.233
pause Command Center reports the rejection
immediately Here is the freshly saved

00:08:19.246 --> 00:08:23.896
Hub response reported is still on desired is
off and the new receipt

00:08:23.908 --> 00:08:28.758
says Failed Its correlation identifier
differs from the paused attempt A later

00:08:28.771 --> 00:08:32.833
agent must preserve this distinction too
requesting the right operation does

00:08:32.846 --> 00:08:34.508
not guarantee that it succeeded

00:08:36.733 --> 00:08:41.446
Recover through a known fixture Reapply
Lights On Reported by a Client Check

00:08:41.458 --> 00:08:45.433
that the controller is healthy and the
original on-in-daylight condition is back

00:08:45.796 --> 00:08:49.646
This reset is a lab operation it is not our
advice for repairing

00:08:49.658 --> 00:08:53.996
real infrastructure It makes the exercise
repeatable and keeps the next lesson's

00:08:54.008 --> 00:08:58.458
starting point honest If a request appears
stuck check service health and whether

00:08:58.471 --> 00:09:02.921
a debugger is paused before clicking again
Inspect the latest receipt before

00:09:02.933 --> 00:09:06.783
repeating a write with an unknown outcome We
end with the original anomaly

00:09:06.796 --> 00:09:10.971
ready for the next investigation A repair in
one lesson does not silently

00:09:10.983 --> 00:09:14.933
disappear between videos we show the reset
that recreates the problem

00:09:16.733 --> 00:09:21.558
Pause the video and do the lab yourself
Prepare the deterministic starting fixture

00:09:21.921 --> 00:09:25.983
Restore the schedule in Command Center and
verify the Hub's state and receipt

00:09:26.346 --> 00:09:30.733
Then prepare Controller Fault repeat the
request and prove that the failure did

00:09:30.746 --> 00:09:35.633
not become a reported repair Finally reset
the original anomaly Your evidence

00:09:35.646 --> 00:09:40.308
note should contain starting facts a
successful command's correlation identifier

00:09:40.633 --> 00:09:44.983
a failed command's correlation identifier
the corresponding reported states and

00:09:44.996 --> 00:09:49.496
the source location that enforces
confirmation The expected successful result

00:09:49.571 --> 00:09:54.721
is desired off reported off override cleared
and Succeeded The failure keeps

00:09:54.733 --> 00:09:59.383
reported on and records Failed Your final
reset should leave the healthy controller

00:09:59.708 --> 00:10:04.433
reported on and override enabled If your
results differ use the receipt detail

00:10:04.446 --> 00:10:08.446
and Aspire logs to explain the difference
before moving on Resume when you

00:10:08.458 --> 00:10:10.296
can explain what each field proves

00:10:12.733 --> 00:10:17.108
Now investigate the code Where is the
scheduled target computed What happens

00:10:17.121 --> 00:10:21.583
if a response says success but omits actual-
state confirmation Where does a stale

00:10:21.596 --> 00:10:26.821
revision get rejected You can consult
documentation or ask an AI assistant but

00:10:26.833 --> 00:10:31.408
require file locations and compare its
claims with your run Finally ask which

00:10:31.421 --> 00:10:34.783
new requirement would justify an agent here
We did not need one to

00:10:34.796 --> 00:10:39.421
perform a fixed well-defined operation We
may need help interpreting a report

00:10:39.796 --> 00:10:44.471
finding evidence choosing among capabilities
or coordinating a longer investigation

00:10:44.833 --> 00:10:49.746
That is the decision map in practice This
lab established the operational foundation

00:10:50.121 --> 00:10:55.208
requests cross a controlled boundary
outcomes are confirmed failures remain
visible

00:10:55.221 --> 00:10:58.771
and the exercise can be reset In the next
module we add an

00:10:58.783 --> 00:11:02.921
investigation agent to this same system The
agent gains a focused way to

00:11:02.933 --> 00:11:06.321
observe the world the Energy Hub keeps
ownership of what is true